  'Anthropological Theory',
  'This course surveys the major theoretical traditions, disputes, and contemporary turns that have shaped anthropology as a discipline. Students read classic and contemporary theory alongside ethnographic cases, learning how concepts such as culture, structure, function, symbol, practice, habitus, discourse, materiality, ontology, race, gender, coloniality, and more-than-human relations become tools for explanation. The course sequence expands into a granular, research-ready syllabus that emphasizes critique, application, and theory-building.',
  $anthjson${"slug": "anthropological-theory", "title": "Anthropological Theory", "description": "This course surveys the major theoretical traditions, disputes, and contemporary turns that have shaped anthropology as a discipline. Students read classic and contemporary theory alongside ethnographic cases, learning how concepts such as culture, structure, function, symbol, practice, habitus, discourse, materiality, ontology, race, gender, coloniality, and more-than-human relations become tools for explanation. The course sequence expands into a granular, research-ready syllabus that emphasizes critique, application, and theory-building.", "topics": [{"id": "1d2992c9-0e41-47f1-a9d7-e841daf78114", "type": "topic", "title": "What Theory Does in Anthropology", "description": "Introduces theory as a toolkit for asking better questions about culture, power, history, meaning, material life, embodiment, and human difference.", "children": [{"id": "49b54260-4f9d-4c1c-b4df-b0a02fb3334b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory, concept, and ethnographic problem", "children": [{"id": "f5c68d25-6b5d-4c13-b90d-9d2aa98892ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept"}, {"id": "8791720b-cfed-4bb4-b08c-98a46623f4ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytic frame"}, {"id": "dc58c191-a854-4bfb-82f0-3c5bc8e0f2c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Problematic"}, {"id": "1aeeee36-2e4b-405c-8262-f830b5afbc59", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnographic case"}, {"id": "e93df49d-2c8a-4abc-a6ae-6abefbae3112", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison"}, {"id": "eed10490-6e1b-4d1f-bbe9-2d7ef1882020", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale"}], "description": "Students learn that theory is not decoration; it defines what counts as a problem, what evidence matters, and what explanation looks like."}, {"id": "dec7f6cb-0b28-4c53-ae74-da87d80c7e13", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Four-field and subfield relationships", "children": [{"id": "c705d51e-551c-453a-bbd8-feeed5e086ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural anthropology"}, {"id": "9a3403c4-999e-4b18-98f8-951c4bf38168", "type": "concept", "title": "Biological anthropology"}, {"id": "f3990041-800f-4ee7-8b53-999c44347fea", "type": "concept", "title": "Archaeology"}, {"id": "30492bf4-5c5a-4549-ba07-ba4d2693d7a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Linguistic anthropology"}, {"id": "361868ea-b76c-4519-b86a-d2a88dfa4113", "type": "concept", "title": "Holism"}, {"id": "17fc0dcd-abf6-4bd9-a5ac-3f3d2a796aa7", "type": "concept", "title": "Interdisciplinarity"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how cultural, biological, archaeological, and linguistic anthropology have different evidence but shared theoretical concerns."}, {"id": "b086b2ac-4190-4ce9-8c79-fe23cba23372", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory and the politics of knowledge", "children": [{"id": "489d0a89-190f-45e3-824a-b10ce86b979f", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemology"}, {"id": "0fc05bd8-7d78-457c-95b6-56363d09dbe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Positionality"}, {"id": "18ec3e43-e57d-4560-815f-ea1baa0b888d", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation"}, {"id": "5de55aff-560e-4a5f-a10f-9d6424053377", "type": "concept", "title": "Knowledge production"}, {"id": "6a2fe460-d942-4455-9495-1fc5e3c90844", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority"}, {"id": "723077f4-1102-4786-b05c-92f9cb509703", "type": "concept", "title": "Public anthropology"}], "description": "Students examine how anthropological knowledge has been shaped by colonial power, race, class, gender, funding, institutions, and publics."}]}, {"id": "9f1b4b66-1c29-4b23-a344-e61270304aad", "type": "topic", "title": "Evolutionism and Early Comparative Thought", "description": "Studies nineteenth-century theories that ranked societies along developmental stages and explains why later anthropology rejected their assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "704a908e-d4ea-4a87-b580-4dcd239979ad", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Unilineal evolutionism", "children": [{"id": "91b81b6b-1fae-4b82-b296-700b563e63aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Savagery/barbarism/civilization"}, {"id": "4db68866-e5d0-4a99-bb97-6c94c82ff127", "type": "concept", "title": "Survivals"}, {"id": "2015850a-347d-4f08-8a03-16741635fcc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Progress narratives"}, {"id": "f0335e03-84a7-4abe-bb24-54adace34245", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnocentrism"}, {"id": "c6a49ef5-c373-43b6-bfe3-08c496725579", "type": "concept", "title": "Armchair anthropology"}], "description": "The point is to understand early anthropology's comparative ambition and its deeply flawed hierarchy of social progress."}, {"id": "ea1aa1d2-4b30-4412-ad7e-46aff8c2d24e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative method and classification", "children": [{"id": "9aeca538-8e1a-41a1-a9ed-05ca0d1b9097", "type": "concept", "title": "Typology"}, {"id": "b50e2b1a-9b90-4bec-b7ae-fc50b4698907", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative tables"}, {"id": "e22cbc10-5759-47bf-af38-b4fe9d33366b", "type": "concept", "title": "Diffusion"}, {"id": "54d7fee5-87d0-4afc-b07d-a783b13e9ef6", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture areas"}, {"id": "afc17a23-6a9e-4616-b812-22ff66c9fe3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Speculative history"}, {"id": "c95195e0-0ea8-49a2-a6e0-1edbc141d7b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Social evolution"}], "description": "Students learn how early theorists used cross-cultural data and why those comparisons often ignored history, context, and power."}, {"id": "93381697-616d-4169-ab57-fc266c1adb50", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Legacy and critique", "children": [{"id": "70ad9c35-722f-4fe1-a975-468196003d3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Scientific racism"}, {"id": "74f93ad8-c666-4a76-8a8a-fbb999f6ffd3", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial knowledge"}, {"id": "7abe7918-6307-41a5-b9ce-620c226dad9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Museum collecting"}, {"id": "97eb39ba-07ff-4489-aa87-e30cda07647d", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural hierarchy"}, {"id": "8302fcd4-3697-46b9-9885-e6e026472921", "type": "concept", "title": "Degeneration theories"}, {"id": "d2e5fd3d-4900-4f70-b8f0-c3bcc15448c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Modern evolutionary anthropology"}], "description": "This subtopic connects early theory to later debates about race science, colonialism, museums, and evolutionary explanation."}]}, {"id": "28bc8254-e221-48b4-9092-a159e5f8b76c", "type": "topic", "title": "Boas, Historical Particularism, and Cultural Relativism", "description": "Explores Boasian anthropology as a decisive critique of racial determinism, grand evolutionism, and decontextualized comparison.", "children": [{"id": "0071e879-3cee-4d0b-a5c0-de0c77f59433", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical particularism", "children": [{"id": "acf1404f-084d-4b8e-ae8a-28848d619b62", "type": "concept", "title": "Diffusion"}, {"id": "63d69bf6-4158-40c9-8c63-533b255bdd7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural history"}, {"id": "0f80aeca-df2d-42e1-b307-c4b421efd394", "type": "concept", "title": "Particularism"}, {"id": "67ffe1e2-d893-4134-b31d-d6d353fba27d", "type": "concept", "title": "Context"}, {"id": "6d178b73-bd2e-4c2f-8f8b-19dac82d8198", "type": "concept", "title": "Contingency"}, {"id": "9d0d99ac-1da1-411c-840e-8a28a1fcf3ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-evolutionism"}], "description": "Students learn to interpret cultural forms through specific histories rather than universal stages."}, {"id": "bd0eb447-67a0-4581-9cb1-accd2a338d3f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cultural relativism", "children": [{"id": "fbcab572-dcd5-4ed8-a8f9-eca4757f6692", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnocentrism"}, {"id": "249262b9-d406-4c3c-be7b-d667a5826ac5", "type": "concept", "title": "Methodological relativism"}, {"id": "cee5b720-ddc4-4317-894f-93275ebad5a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral judgment"}, {"id": "4e89e37f-a17e-4bd0-92d2-2f476d441876", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation"}, {"id": "8919b354-50be-49c9-b9d0-ce83693759ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural critique"}, {"id": "5288eb4a-9ac1-4457-80ac-dcc5570aaa02", "type": "concept", "title": "Human rights debate"}], "description": "This subtopic distinguishes methodological relativism from moral relativism and shows why the distinction matters."}, {"id": "37503b73-e2c7-4988-ad0e-91eb3dcb24b0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Boasian legacies", "children": [{"id": "ce06e273-4b79-45e2-9060-aeee31b763b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Race critique"}, {"id": "d5e764e4-a9f8-4c75-9353-4ee671cb4144", "type": "concept", "title": "Sapir"}, {"id": "b64924d3-29c4-4f01-9da9-9f2a7528f018", "type": "concept", "title": "Benedict"}, {"id": "892fd48e-a321-4149-aaab-d127db5a37c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Mead"}, {"id": "5681a34d-024a-4016-9e42-020a804aaeb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture and personality"}, {"id": "0fb4e33f-3fe8-47ba-9dc5-4e8200e748d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Language documentation"}], "description": "Students trace Boas's influence on race critique, salvage anthropology, linguistics, folklore, and American cultural anthropology."}]}, {"id": "2adb5347-670c-4558-8f17-67e74649b7c8", "type": "topic", "title": "Functionalism and Structural Functionalism", "description": "Examines theories that explain institutions by their roles in satisfying needs, maintaining order, regulating conflict, and reproducing social structure.", "children": [{"id": "32bf0804-6cd1-47c5-97ef-855103740897", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Malinowski and functionalism", "children": [{"id": "3da93e4f-196e-4042-bffa-df70af81cd91", "type": "concept", "title": "Participant observation"}, {"id": "cfd89e88-626c-4fa1-8035-8024d0631af7", "type": "concept", "title": "Needs theory"}, {"id": "a9ec0cc8-f675-4a6d-8830-5e2a0fa0feb9", "type": "concept", "title": "Institution"}, {"id": "b53b0b97-87f9-4062-9b48-6df5e9574fa3", "type": "concept", "title": "Trobriand Islands"}, {"id": "e2ff5ce0-f5d6-49d5-85be-71e3d1826676", "type": "concept", "title": "Kula exchange"}, {"id": "555a6345-7a0d-4df4-844c-26dc384d89d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnographic present"}], "description": "Students learn how intensive fieldwork and the concept of function changed the discipline's method and theory."}, {"id": "3ad61422-cab5-4ff6-a919-db6e205984c0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Radcliffe-Brown and social structure", "children": [{"id": "e22c9473-bd14-4f63-adf9-9ae1c0ad212f", "type": "concept", "title": "Social structure"}, {"id": "60f5e10a-adbc-44aa-a021-1c6e29ddc13e", "type": "concept", "title": "Social function"}, {"id": "1eead2b9-a652-4ae3-9604-5eb72eca4676", "type": "concept", "title": "Jural rules"}, {"id": "d1aa2bda-be24-4454-8706-7abd86ca1ff5", "type": "concept", "title": "Descent"}, {"id": "14a91f4d-f6ed-422a-b3ef-35fd84225e5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Equilibrium"}, {"id": "d4109d54-9b1b-48a6-b466-d2aba0c37192", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative sociology"}], "description": "This subtopic emphasizes relations, roles, norms, and institutions as parts of a social system."}, {"id": "42c22930-1e0f-4f34-aabd-c5b9b417c7d0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critiques of functionalism", "children": [{"id": "25cc09c8-3c70-44cb-aca9-b43cdb263c60", "type": "concept", "title": "Ahistoricism"}, {"id": "052678b2-5769-4caa-959d-f84defb5c79a", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict"}, {"id": "07c7fbd1-531d-48e5-8a45-e297aa7f4301", "type": "concept", "title": "Change"}, {"id": "6c1691cf-0ecd-4452-b87e-6db26e7ac8d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial context"}, {"id": "28e4ebca-f9b5-4175-a963-c5575172b1db", "type": "concept", "title": "Teleology"}, {"id": "2feb758c-8da5-476d-a715-9e958aa6f5af", "type": "concept", "title": "Over-integration"}], "description": "Students identify the limits of explaining institutions as stable, integrated, and consensual."}]}, {"id": "61d1dbdf-222d-4fd6-933d-3eb82891eab6", "type": "topic", "title": "Structuralism, Symbolic Anthropology, and Meaning", "description": "Develops tools for interpreting symbols, myths, rituals, classifications, and cultural systems of meaning.", "children": [{"id": "89e0d0e6-